==============================================================================
Requirements                                   *kkiyama117-nvim-requirements*

Required: >
	git	plugin installs (dpp-protocol-git) and the bootloader's
		recovery clones
	deno	denops runtime (installed via mise)
	mise	manages deno and the LSP server binaries
		(~/.config/mise/config.toml) — mason.nvim is NOT used
<
Optional: >
	kakehashi	LSP bridge server binary
			(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp.txt|); install with
			`deno task install-external-bin kakehashi`
			in scripts/install_external_bin/
	tree-sitter	CLI for tree-sitter-manager.nvim
	rg		ripgrep, used by ddu grep sources
	mocword		ddc-source-mocword (needs $MOCWORD_DATA)
	SKK dictionary	for skkeleton Japanese input
	PlemolJP font	GUI font (neovide)
<
Environment variables:

	$NVIM_CONFIG_HOME	set by init.lua to the config directory
	$NVIM_CACHE_HOME	set by init.lua to $XDG_CACHE_HOME/nvim;
				holds the dpp cache, undo/backup/swap and
				logs
	$NVIM_DEBUG		"true" enables debug mode: verbose logging
				and notify/messages dumps under
				$NVIM_CACHE_HOME/logs/
	$GITHUB_API_TOKEN	raises the GitHub API rate limit of
				dpp-ext-installer

==============================================================================
Install                                             *kkiyama117-nvim-install*

1. Fetch: >
	git clone https://github.com/kkiyama117/nvim_config.git ~/.config/nvim
<
2. Run: >
	nvim
<
That is all. On first start `lua/bootloader` installs everything by
itself: minimum deps (dpp.vim, dpp-ext-lazy, denops.vim), then the
dpp-ext plugins, then all user plugins. Each layer has a fallback that
clones missing repositories and rebuilds the plugin state, asking to
restart when needed. See |kkiyama117-nvim-bootloader| for the full
flow and its failure modes.

==============================================================================
Directory layout                                     *kkiyama117-nvim-layout*
>
	init.lua	entry point: sets $NVIM_CONFIG_HOME /
			$NVIM_CACHE_HOME, minimal runtimepath, then
			calls require('bootloader').startup()
	lua/bootloader/	dpp bootstrap and recovery
			(|kkiyama117-nvim-bootloader|)
	lua/vimrc/	core vimrc modules (options, mappings, commands,
			utils, ...), compiled into the dpp state as
			inlineVimrcs; nvim/ and unix/ (or windows/)
			subdirectories are platform variants
	lua/hooks/	per-plugin dpp hook files (*.dpp) and
			heirline_components/ (statusline modules)
	deps/		dpp plugin lists (TOML); deps/nvim/ holds
			nvim-only lists (lsp, treesitter, ui, ...)
	denops/		TypeScript configs of dpp/ddc/ddu/ddt
	after/		ftplugin/dpp.lua, lsp/kakehashi.lua,
			tree-sitter queries
	ftdetect/	custom `dpp` filetype for the hook files
	syntax/		(lua base + hook marker highlighting)
	plugins/	local development plugins, scanned by
			dpp-ext-local
	scripts/	install_external_bin Deno CLI: installs binaries
			declared as `external_commands` in the TOMLs
			from GitHub releases into ~/.local/bin
	doc/		this documentation (doc/agents/ is for AI
			agents only)
<
==============================================================================
Plugin management                                       *kkiyama117-nvim-dpp*

Plugins are managed by dpp.vim, configured in `denops/dpp.ts`.

Plugin lists ~

Every plugin is an entry in `deps/**/*.toml` (dpp-ext-toml). All TOMLs
are loaded lazily except `dpp.toml` and `merge.toml`; individual
entries opt out with `lazy = false` or trigger on `on_event` /
`on_ft` / `on_cmd` / `on_map` / `on_source` / `depends`.

Hook files ~

Per-plugin configuration lives in `lua/hooks/<plugin>.dpp`, referenced
from the TOML as `hooks_file`. A `.dpp` file is Lua split into dpp hook
blocks by comment markers: >
	-- lua_add {{{
	...
	-- }}}
	-- lua_source {{{
	...
	-- }}}
<
The custom `dpp` filetype provides highlighting, tree-sitter lua
injection, LSP support and marker folding for these files
(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-dpp-filetype|).

Inline vimrcs ~

`lua/vimrc/*` (plus the platform subdirectories) are compiled into the
dpp state as inlineVimrcs, so they load from the cached state, not as
individual files.

Updates ~

Writing any config file under $NVIM_CONFIG_HOME triggers a
BufWritePost watcher that installs missing plugins, rebuilds the state
with `dpp#make_state()` and offers a restart. Manual full update: >
	:DppUpdate
<
==============================================================================
Key mappings                                       *kkiyama117-nvim-mappings*

Defined in `lua/vimrc/mappings.lua`; plugin-dependent mappings live in
the plugin's own `lua/hooks/*.dpp`. Conventions:

- mapleader is <Space>, maplocalleader is `,`.
- `ZZ ZQ M Q` and the arrow keys are disabled; `s`, `;`, `q` are
  turned into prefixes; `;:` gives the original `:`.
- <Leader> keys remap to named pseudo-key prefixes: >
	<Leader>a	[AGENTIC]	agentic.nvim
	<Leader>d	[DP]		ddu / ddt launchers
	<Leader>g	[GIT]		vim-gin
	<Leader>w	[WINDOW]	same as CTRL-W
	<Leader>T	[TOGGLE]	option toggles:
			a autoread, b background transparency
			(|kkiyama117-nvim-background|), c conceal,
			h hit-enter prompt, s spell, w wrap
<
- `;` is the ddu prefix (`;b` buffers, `;f` files, `;g*` git, ...);
  `s` is reserved for ddu/ddt as well.
- `q` is the quit/macro prefix: `qm`/`qM` are the only macro
  registers, `qq` smart quit, `qt` tabclose, `qr` restart.
- which-key pops up on <Leader>; `<Leader>?` shows it explicitly.

==============================================================================
Colorscheme interface                           *kkiyama117-nvim-colorscheme*

`lua/vimrc/colorscheme.lua` declares a small contract that every
colorscheme hook must fulfill, at source time and again on every
|ColorScheme| event: >
	g:vimrc#colors		the scheme's palette table
	g:vimrc#color_bg	the scheme's default background color
<
Consumers: the heirline components
(lua/hooks/heirline_components/colors.lua), cmdline.vim, and the
background transparency toggle (|kkiyama117-nvim-background|).

Candidates are listed in `deps/colorscheme.toml` (tokyonight,
nightfox, catppuccin, kanagawa); catppuccin is the one whose hook
actually calls `:colorscheme`.

==============================================================================
Commands                                      *kkiyama117-nvim-commands-list*

:DppUpdate		force a full plugin update and state rebuild
			(|kkiyama117-nvim-dpp|)

Encoding / format commands from `lua/vimrc/commands.lua`:

:Utf8 :Iso2022jp :Cp932 :Euc
			reopen the current file with `++enc=...`
:WUtf8 :WCp932		set 'fileencoding' for the next write
:WUnix :WDos		write with `++ff=...` and reload

See also |kkiyama117-nvim-commands.txt|.

